desc:Variable Ratio Compressor (Magnolius)
// @description Variable Ratio Compressor (Magnolius)
// @author Magnolius
// @version 1.2
// @link GitHub https://github.com/m-dahlberg/magnolius-reaper
// @provides
//   gui_kit/gui_kit.jsfx-inc
//   gui_kit/*.png
//   Magnolius_VariableRatioCompressor.help.txt
// @about
//   A compressor that solves for the ratio each passage needs instead of being
//   told one. Lookahead finds the loudest upcoming peak, derives the exact
//   ratio that lands it on the output ceiling, then holds that ratio across
//   the passage.
//
//   Everything between threshold and ceiling is compressed at the same
//   currently-valid ratio, so relative dynamics inside the passage survive
//   instead of only the tips being flattened.
//
//   A sliding-min plus double-boxcar smoother means the gain never overshoots,
//   so the ceiling is held exactly whenever the ratio the passage needs is
//   within Max Ratio. Past that the ratio is capped and peaks come through.
//
//   A four-band version is available as Variable Ratio Compressor MB.
//
//   Licensed GPL-3.0-or-later.
// @changelog
//   New interface built on the Magnolius GUI kit: knobs with typed value
//   fields, the scrolling display in a framed plot, a live transfer curve that
//   bends as the ratio solves, GR and ratio meters, a scale selector shared
//   with the other Magnolius plugins, and a built-in help panel.
//
//   The scrolling display now plots dB rather than linear amplitude, with the
//   threshold and the ceiling drawn on it as real reference lines. On the old
//   linear scale everything below about -30 dB was a hairline on the centre
//   line, which is most of what a threshold down at -60 is actually working on.
//
//   New Display History control (1-30 s) for the scroll window, which used to
//   be hardwired to about 5 s.
//
//   Max Ratio now runs 1:1 to 10:1 in steps of 0.1, instead of 2:1 to 1000:1
//   in steps of 1. On the old range the musically useful part of the control
//   lived in the first few per cent of its travel, which a knob makes far
//   worse than a slider did, and the old floor of 2:1 ruled out the gentle
//   ratios this compressor is often wanted for. 1:1 is a valid setting: it
//   pins the solver at unity, so the compression is off while Blend and Output
//   Gain still work.
//
//   A project saved with Max Ratio above 10 loads clamped to 10, which will
//   compress less than it used to - raise the threshold or lower the ceiling
//   to compensate.
//
//   The default moves from 200 to 3. The old default was chosen to be out of
//   the way - at 200:1 the clamp effectively never engaged and the ceiling was
//   always held by the ratio. 3:1 is a real setting rather than a disabled one,
//   so a new instance now sounds like a compressor from the first note. Raise
//   it towards 10:1 for the old behaviour. Existing projects keep their own
//   saved value; only new instances see this.
//
//   Simplified: the safety clipper, the Safety Clip switch, the Makeup Position
//   switch and the Out Peak readout are all gone.
//
//   The ratio trace and the RATIO meter are now on a FIXED 1:1 to 10:1 scale
//   instead of one that followed Max Ratio. Following it meant the same ratio
//   drew at a different height as Max Ratio moved, which reads as the ratio
//   having changed when nothing about the audio did - the same reason the
//   gain-reduction overlay has always been pinned to 0-24 dB.
//
//   Their slider NUMBERS (6 and 10) are left as gaps rather than renumbering
//   the rest down. A project stores slider values positionally by slider
//   number, so this keeps every remaining control loading into itself. REAPER
//   does however compact the PARAMETER indices it exposes to automation, so an
//   existing envelope on Lookahead, Ratio Release, Max Ratio or Display History
//   now points one parameter too high and needs re-pointing. That is
//   unavoidable when a parameter is removed - the gaps do not change it.
//
//   Output Gain (was Makeup Gain) is now ALWAYS applied last, after the blend,
//   and is no longer part of the ratio solve. The old Pre position folded it
//   into the solve so the compressor worked harder to hold the ceiling despite
//   it; that behaviour no longer exists. A project saved in Pre will now be
//   louder and less compressed than it was - it now behaves as if it had been
//   saved in Post, which was the default.
//
//   Without the clipper, Output Ceiling is no longer an absolute guarantee. It
//   is hit exactly while the required ratio is within Max Ratio, and peaks pass
//   once the ratio is capped - which the new 3:1 default makes an everyday
//   case, not a rare one. Put a limiter after this if you need a true ceiling.
//
//   Input Gain is unchanged, and remains a genuine pre-compressor drive: the
//   detector, the curve and the dry half of the blend all see it.
//
//   Alt-drag on Input Gain or Output Gain moves the other one the opposite way
//   by the same number of dB, so you can change how hard the compressor works
//   without changing the level. It works on the knob, the wheel and the value
//   field, and each value still clamps to its own range.

// Program-dependent compressor: lookahead finds the loudest upcoming peak,
// derives the exact ratio that lands that peak on the output ceiling, then
// holds that ratio across the passage. Everything between threshold and
// ceiling is compressed at the same (currently valid) ratio, so relative
// dynamics inside the passage survive instead of only the tips being flattened.
//
// Signal flow:
//   env -> sliding max (lookahead) -> required ratio -> ratio ballistics
//       -> static curve applied to instantaneous env -> gain
//       -> sliding min (lookahead) -> 2x boxcar -> applied to delayed audio
//
// The sliding-min + double-boxcar pair is what stops the gain overshooting: at
// the output instant of any peak, every gain sample entering the smoother has
// already "seen" that peak, so the smoothed gain can never be higher than the
// gain that peak required. That makes the ceiling exact WHENEVER THE SOLVER IS
// ALLOWED TO REACH IT - i.e. while the required ratio is within Max Ratio.
// Once Max Ratio clamps, the gain is no longer the gain the peak asked for and
// the peak comes through. There is no longer a clipper behind this to catch it
// (removed in 1.2), so Output Ceiling is a target the compressor hits exactly
// until it runs out of ratio, not an absolute guarantee.
//
// THREADING - do not undo this.  Every @gfx variable is g_-prefixed and no
// name is used on both sides.  The rule is not "@gfx calls no functions": it
// is that NO FUNCTION IS CALLED FROM BOTH THREADS.  EEL2 function locals AND
// parameters are static and shared, so a function entered from @gfx while the
// audio thread is inside it stomps the audio thread's copy of every local
// mid-sample.  So:
//   audio thread only:  lin2db, update_params
//   @gfx only:          accent, ui_scale, count_missing_images, every gk_*
// That is also why the transfer curve inlines the compressor maths instead of
// sharing a function with @sample, and why a widget change sets need_update
// rather than calling update_params() from the UI thread - @block picks the
// flag up on the audio thread, so the new coefficients do not depend on REAPER
// re-running @slider after slider_automate (which it does not).  It looks like
// duplication; it is not.
//
// The same reasoning runs the other way for DRAWING.  @block does not run on a
// stopped track, so @gfx must not read the audio thread's derived values or
// every reference line and the whole transfer curve freeze until the user
// presses play.  @gfx therefore derives g_thr / g_ceil / g_blend from the raw
// slider variables itself, at the top of the section.  Those three lines
// duplicate update_params() on purpose - keep them in step.
//
// EEL2 identifiers are case-insensitive: W and w are ONE variable.  The
// detector window length is the global W, so nothing in @gfx may use a bare
// w - hence the g_ prefix on every last one of them.
//
// v1.2

// Shared memory, namespaced to this vendor so it cannot collide with other
// plugins' gmem. Slot map (keep in step across every options:gmem=Magnolius
// plugin - they share the one map):
//   gmem[0]  interface scale, as index+1 (0 = not set yet)
options:gmem=Magnolius

// Every slider name carries a leading '-' so REAPER's generic control panel
// stays hidden behind the custom interface, while the parameters remain
// automatable and visible to the render tests.
slider1:s_in=0<-12,24,0.1>-Input Gain (dB)
slider2:s_ceil=-0.3<-24,0,0.05>-Output Ceiling (dB)
slider3:s_thr=-18<-60,0,0.1>-Threshold (dB)
slider4:s_blend=100<0,100,0.1>-Blend (% wet)
slider5:s_comp=0<-20,20,0.1>-Output Gain (dB)
// slider6 was Makeup Position and slider10 was Safety Clip. Both are gone,
// and their NUMBERS ARE LEFT AS GAPS on purpose: a JSFX project stores slider
// values positionally, so renumbering 7-11 down into the holes would load an
// old project's Lookahead into Ratio Release and so on, silently.
slider7:s_la=5<0.5,20,0.1>-Lookahead (ms)
slider8:s_rel=200<10,2000,1>-Ratio Release (ms)
slider9:s_maxr=3<1,10,0.1>-Max Ratio (:1)
slider11:s_hist=6<1,30,0.5>-Display History (s)

in_pin:left
in_pin:right
out_pin:left
out_pin:right

import gui_kit/gui_kit.jsfx-inc

// A filename: line takes the WHOLE rest of the line as the path, so a trailing
// // comment becomes part of the filename and the image silently fails to load
// (the slot just reports 0 x 0). Keep these lines bare.
//   0 knob, Dynamics (cyan)   1 knob, Gain (yellow)
//   2 knob, Gain bipolar (Output Gain)      12 help text, last slot
filename:0,gui_kit/knob_cyan_grid.png
filename:1,gui_kit/knob_yellow_grid.png
filename:2,gui_kit/knob_yellow_bipolar_grid.png
filename:3,gui_kit/value_field.png
filename:4,gui_kit/header_bar.png
filename:5,gui_kit/panel_tile.png
filename:6,gui_kit/dropdown.png
filename:7,gui_kit/button_cyan.png
filename:8,gui_kit/wave_frame_9slice.png
filename:9,gui_kit/playhead_line_cyan.png
filename:10,gui_kit/playhead_marker_cyan.png
filename:11,gui_kit/logo_magnolius_grey.png
filename:12,Magnolius_VariableRatioCompressor.help.txt

@init

// @init re-runs on samplerate change and on recompile.  ext_noinit stops the
// other trigger - transport start - so the detector deques, the delay line and
// the scrolling history survive hitting play instead of being wiped every time.
ext_noinit = 1;

DMAX    = 8192;              // must be a power of two
MASK    = DMAX - 1;
LN10_20 = 0.11512925464970229;   // ln(10)/20   dB -> linear exponent
DB20    = 8.6858896380650366;    // 20/ln(10)   ln  -> dB
TINY    = 10^(-30);          // EEL2 has no scientific notation: 1e-30 is a
                             // silent syntax error that kills the section

dlyL = 0;
dlyR = dlyL + DMAX;          // lookahead audio delay
mxv  = dlyR + DMAX;          // max-deque values
mxi  = mxv  + DMAX;          // max-deque sample indices
mnv  = mxi  + DMAX;          // min-deque values
mni  = mnv  + DMAX;          // min-deque sample indices
ma1  = mni  + DMAX;          // boxcar stage 1
ma2  = ma1  + DMAX;          // boxcar stage 2

// ---- scrolling display history ----
// One column per col_len samples, holding the peak dB of the (delayed,
// time-aligned) input and of the output, the deepest gain reduction and the
// highest ratio reached in that column.  @gfx only reads; @sample only writes.
// Power of two so the ring index is a mask, not a modulo.
HIST_N   = 1024;
HMASK    = HIST_N - 1;
HIST_IN  = ma2      + DMAX;
HIST_OUT = HIST_IN  + HIST_N;
HIST_GR  = HIST_OUT + HIST_N;
HIST_RT  = HIST_GR  + HIST_N;

// Display scales.  All three are fixed on purpose: a scale that followed a
// slider would make the same event draw a different size as the slider moved,
// which reads as the event having changed when it has not.
FLOORDB      = -66;          // waveform plot: centre line, 0 dB at the edges
GR_FULL_DB   = 24;           // gain-reduction overlay and meter, full scale
RATIO_FULL   = 10;           // ratio trace and meter, full scale (1:1 at the
                             // bottom edge).  This MUST match slider9's maximum
                             // or the trace stops short of the top, or clips
                             // against it, with no error anywhere.
LN_RATIO     = log(RATIO_FULL);
CURVE_LO     = -60;          // transfer-curve panel, dB in
CURVE_HI     = 12;           // transfer-curve panel, dB out headroom

rs         = 1;
lastla     = -1;
need_reset = 1;
mtr        = 1;
rmtr       = 1;

// Display readouts, converted once per block rather than per sample, so @gfx
// never has to call lin2db (see the threading note).
gr_db  = 0;
rt_now  = 1;

// Set by @gfx when a widget moves a slider, consumed by @block on the audio
// thread.  Never call update_params() from @gfx.
need_update = 0;

function lin2db(l) ( log(max(l, TINY)) * DB20 );

// Everything derived from the sliders lives here so @slider and @block cannot
// disagree, and so behaviour never depends on REAPER re-running @slider after
// slider_automate.  Idempotent, and audio thread only.
function update_params()
(
  ceil_db = s_ceil;
  thr_db  = min(s_thr, s_ceil - 0.1);   // threshold must sit below the ceiling
  blend   = s_blend * 0.01;
  maxr    = s_maxr;

  ceil_lin = exp(ceil_db * LN10_20);
  ig       = exp(s_in * LN10_20);
  mk       = exp(s_comp * LN10_20);

  la = floor(s_la * 0.001 * srate + 0.5);
  la < 1 ? la = 1;
  la > DMAX - 8 ? la = DMAX - 8;
  W = la + 1;                            // detector window length

  box = floor(la * 0.5);
  box < 1 ? box = 1;                     // two cascaded boxcars, total span ~la

  relc = exp(-1 / max(1, s_rel * 0.001 * srate));

  pdc_delay  = la;
  pdc_bot_ch = 0;
  pdc_top_ch = 2;
  ext_tail_size = la + srate * 0.5;

  col_len = max(1, floor(srate * s_hist / HIST_N));

  la != lastla ? ( lastla = la; need_reset = 1; );

  need_reset ? (
    memset(dlyL, 0, DMAX * 2);           // dlyL and dlyR are contiguous
    memset(ma1,  1, DMAX);
    memset(ma2,  1, DMAX);
    sum1 = box; sum2 = box;
    b1p  = 0;   b2p  = 0;
    mxh  = 0; mxt = 0; mxc = 0;
    mnh  = 0; mnt = 0; mnc = 0;
    n    = 0;
    wp   = 0;
    rp   = DMAX - la;
    rs   = 1;

    memset(HIST_IN, FLOORDB - 200, HIST_N * 2);   // IN and OUT are contiguous
    memset(HIST_GR, 0, HIST_N);
    memset(HIST_RT, 1, HIST_N);
    hpos = 0;
    acc_in = 0; acc_out = 0; acc_gr = 1; acc_rt = 1; acc_n = 0;

    need_reset = 0;
  );
);

update_params();

// ==========================================================================
//  Interface
// ==========================================================================
gk_init();
gk_img_field = 3; gk_img_header = 4; gk_img_panel = 5;
gk_img_dropdown = 6; gk_img_button = 7;
gk_img_wave_frame = 8; gk_img_playhead_line = 9; gk_img_playhead_marker = 10;

// REAPER refuses to load an image taller than somewhere between 6144 and 7680
// px, and returns a zero-size image rather than an error - a filmstrip widget
// then draws nothing while still responding to the mouse.  Width is not
// affected.  So the knob strips are packed as grids: 64 frames in 8 columns
// (1024 x 1024).  The count lives in two places, here and in the PNG; change
// one without the other and the knob draws the wrong frame, silently.
gk_knob_cols = 8; gk_knob_frames = 64;

// Counts image slots REAPER would not load.  It reports a zero-size image and
// no error, and every filmstrip widget then falls back to vector drawing, so
// without this a failed load is completely silent and passes for working art.
// By far the most likely cause is a bad path - and note that a filename: line
// takes the whole rest of the line, so a trailing comment silently breaks it.
// @gfx only.
function count_missing_images(cnt) local(i, iw, ih, bad)
(
  i = 0; bad = 0;
  loop(cnt, gfx_getimgdim(i, iw, ih); iw <= 0 ? bad += 1; i += 1;);
  bad;
);

// Section accent, applied to everything the kit draws with primitives.  It
// persists between frames, so each section sets it before drawing.  @gfx only.
function accent(ar, ag, ab) ( gk_ac_r = ar; gk_ac_g = ag; gk_ac_b = ab; );

// ---- help viewer ----
// The panel is drawn by the widget library; the plugin only owns the state.
// The help file is the last filename: slot, after the images, so the
// missing-image count is not thrown off by it.
HELP_FILE = 12;
HELP_BASE = 300;          // first string slot used for help text
HELP_MAX  = 600;          // most lines kept
help_open = 0; help_lines = 0;

// ---- interface scale ----
// The layout is authored at UI_W x UI_H logical px and every coordinate runs
// through gk_S, so scaling the whole interface - drawing, blits, hit-testing
// and drag deltas alike - is a matter of setting gk_S.  JSFX cannot resize its
// own window (@gfx W H is a compile-time literal and gfx_w/gfx_h are
// read-only), so this scales the content inside whatever window REAPER gives
// us; "Fit" tracks the window, a fixed percentage does not.  Keep UI_W/UI_H in
// step with the @gfx line: 720x488 is 100%, so 125% wants 900x610 of window
// and 150% wants 1080x732.
UI_W = 720; UI_H = 488;
LOGO_ASPECT = 512/101;    // logo_magnolius_grey.png
GM_UI_SCALE = 0;          // gmem slot holding the shared scale

// The scale is global: every instance reads it from gmem each frame, so
// changing it in one window changes all open windows and any instance added
// later in this session.  Each instance also serialises its own copy, which
// seeds the global when the first one loads in a fresh REAPER session.
// gmem is session-scoped: REAPER frees it once the last instance is removed,
// and a JSFX cannot write a settings file, so the scale cannot be persisted
// from in here.  100%, not Fit: the @gfx line already asks for exactly the
// design size, so 100% is pixel-exact on a fresh window and the artwork is
// blitted 1:1, where Fit softens everything by a fractional factor as soon as
// REAPER restores the window at some other size.  Keep this in step across
// every options:gmem=Magnolius plugin - whichever opens first in a session
// seeds the shared slot.
UI_SCALE_DEFAULT = 1;
gui_scale_idx = UI_SCALE_DEFAULT;
function ui_scale(idx) ( idx == 1 ? 1 : idx == 2 ? 1.25 : idx == 3 ? 1.5 : idx == 4 ? 2 : 0; );

@slider

update_params();

@block

// The UI thread only ever raises this flag; the derived values are rebuilt
// here, on the audio thread, so a widget change never enters update_params()
// underneath @sample.  See the threading note at the top of the file.
need_update ? ( need_update = 0; update_params(); );

// Display readouts, converted once per block rather than per sample.  @gfx
// reads these numbers and never calls lin2db itself.
gr_db  = -lin2db(mtr);
rt_now  = rmtr;

@sample

// Input gain drives the entire plugin. The detector, the curve and the dry side
// of the blend all see the boosted signal, so this pushes density, not level.
l = spl0 * ig;
r = spl1 * ig;

dlyL[wp] = l;
dlyR[wp] = r;

a = max(abs(l), abs(r));     // stereo-linked peak detector

// ---- sliding maximum over the lookahead window (monotonic deque) ----
while (mxc > 0 && mxv[(mxt + MASK) & MASK] <= a) (
  mxt = (mxt + MASK) & MASK;
  mxc -= 1;
);
mxv[mxt] = a;
mxi[mxt] = n;
mxt = (mxt + 1) & MASK;
mxc += 1;
while (mxi[mxh] <= n - W) ( mxh = (mxh + 1) & MASK; mxc -= 1; );
pk = mxv[mxh];

// ---- ratio required to land that peak exactly on the ceiling ----
// Blend and gain compensation are solved for, not bolted on afterwards:
//   y = x*(1-blend) + x*g*mk*blend   and we want |y| = ceil at the peak
//   => g = (ceil/pk - (1-blend)) / (blend*mk)
// then invert the compressor curve to find the ratio that produces that g:
//   g_dB = -(P-T)*(1 - 1/R)   =>   R = 1 / (1 + g_dB/(P-T))
rt = 1;
pk > 0.0000001 ? (
  pk_db = log(pk) * DB20;
  pk_db > thr_db ? (
    greq = blend > 0.0001 ? (ceil_lin / pk - (1 - blend)) / blend : 2;
    greq < 1 ? (
      greq < 0.000001 ? greq = 0.000001;
      gdb  = log(greq) * DB20;
      den  = pk_db - thr_db;
      rden = 1 + gdb / den;
      rt   = rden > 0.000001 ? 1 / rden : maxr;   // <=0 means even inf:1 is short
      rt > maxr ? rt = maxr;
      rt < 1    ? rt = 1;
    );
  );
);

// ---- ratio ballistics: instant up (the sliding max already looked ahead),
//      timed release. This hold is what makes the ratio program-dependent. ----
rt > rs ? rs = rt : rs = rt + (rs - rt) * relc;

// ---- static curve, applied to the instantaneous envelope ----
g = 1;
a > 0.0000001 ? (
  a_db = log(a) * DB20;
  a_db > thr_db ? (
    g = exp( -(a_db - thr_db) * (1 - 1/rs) * LN10_20 );
  );
);

// ---- sliding minimum of the gain over the same window ----
while (mnc > 0 && mnv[(mnt + MASK) & MASK] >= g) (
  mnt = (mnt + MASK) & MASK;
  mnc -= 1;
);
mnv[mnt] = g;
mni[mnt] = n;
mnt = (mnt + 1) & MASK;
mnc += 1;
while (mni[mnh] <= n - W) ( mnh = (mnh + 1) & MASK; mnc -= 1; );
gm = mnv[mnh];

// ---- two cascaded boxcars: C1-continuous gain, no added audio latency ----
b1p += 1; b1p >= box ? b1p = 0;
sum1 += gm - ma1[b1p]; ma1[b1p] = gm;
s1 = sum1 / box;

b2p += 1; b2p >= box ? b2p = 0;
sum2 += s1 - ma2[b2p]; ma2[b2p] = s1;
gs = sum2 / box;

gs > 1 ? gs = 1;             // guards long-run running-sum drift
gs < 0 ? gs = 0;

// ---- output ----
xl = dlyL[rp];
xr = dlyR[rp];

ol  = xl * (1 - blend) + xl * gs * blend;
orr = xr * (1 - blend) + xr * gs * blend;

// Output gain is always the last thing that happens, after the blend, and it
// is NOT part of the ratio solve - so it moves the level and nothing else.
// The compressor holds the peak on the ceiling internally and this trims the
// result, which lands the output at (ceiling + output gain).
ol  *= mk;
orr *= mk;

spl0 = ol;
spl1 = orr;

wp += 1; wp >= DMAX ? wp = 0;
rp += 1; rp >= DMAX ? rp = 0;
n  += 1;

gs   < mtr  ? mtr  = gs : mtr  = gs + (mtr  - gs)  * 0.9995;
rs   > rmtr ? rmtr = rs : rmtr = rs + (rmtr - rs) * 0.9995;

// ---- display history ----
// xl/xr are the DELAYED input, so they line up exactly with ol/orr and the
// scope shows cause and effect on the same column.  Converted to dB here, on
// the audio thread, so @gfx only ever reads numbers.
pkin  = max(abs(xl),  abs(xr));
pkout = max(abs(ol),  abs(orr));

acc_in  = max(acc_in,  pkin);
acc_out = max(acc_out, pkout);
acc_gr  = min(acc_gr,  gs);
acc_rt  = max(acc_rt,  rs);

acc_n += 1;
acc_n >= col_len ? (
  HIST_IN[hpos]  = lin2db(acc_in);
  HIST_OUT[hpos] = lin2db(acc_out);
  HIST_GR[hpos]  = -lin2db(acc_gr);     // positive dB of reduction
  HIST_RT[hpos]  = acc_rt;
  hpos = (hpos + 1) & HMASK;
  acc_in = 0; acc_out = 0; acc_gr = 1; acc_rt = 1; acc_n = 0;
);

@gfx 720 488

// Reminder: every local here is g_-prefixed, nothing calls a function the
// audio thread also calls, and the transfer-curve maths is a deliberate copy
// of the @sample curve.  See the threading note at the top of the file.
// EEL2 is case-insensitive, so a bare w, n, a, g or r here would silently
// overwrite an audio-thread global mid-sample.

gk_begin();
g_chg = 0;

// Take the scale from the shared global, or seed it if nothing has yet.
gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] > 0 ? gui_scale_idx = gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] - 1
                      : gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] = gui_scale_idx + 1;

// gk_begin set gk_S from gfx_ext_retina (1 or 2). Fixed percentages are taken
// relative to that, so 100% stays pixel-exact on a HiDPI display; Fit divides
// the physical window by the design size, which already includes it.
g_ret = gk_S;
g_scl = ui_scale(gui_scale_idx);
gk_S = max(0.5, g_scl > 0 ? g_ret * g_scl : min(gfx_w / UI_W, gfx_h / UI_H));
gfx_setfont(1, gk_font, 11*gk_S);
gfx_setfont(2, gk_font, 13*gk_S, 'b');
gfx_setfont(1);

// Display values, derived HERE from the raw slider variables rather than read
// from the audio thread's copies.  This matters: slider_automate() does not
// re-run @slider, so a widget change reaches update_params() only through the
// need_update flag that @block picks up - and @BLOCK DOES NOT RUN ON A STOPPED
// TRACK.  Reading thr_db / ceil_db / blend here would leave the
// threshold line, the ceiling line and the whole transfer curve frozen at their
// old values while the knob visibly moved, until the user pressed play.  These
// six lines are a deliberate duplicate of the corresponding lines in
// update_params(); keep the two in step.  The audio thread is unaffected -
// @block always runs before the @sample calls it precedes, so the DSP never
// processes a sample with stale coefficients.
g_thr   = min(s_thr, s_ceil - 0.1);
g_ceil  = s_ceil;
g_blend = s_blend * 0.01;

gk_header(0, 10, 10, 700, 22, "VARIABLE RATIO COMPRESSOR", -1, 0);

// Scale selector and help button, far right of the header. Their x is pinned
// to the right edge of what is actually on screen, so a scale too large for
// the window can never push these controls out of reach.
g_rr = min(UI_W - 20, gfx_w/gk_S - 10);
g_dx = g_rr - 86;
g_i = gk_dropdown(40, g_dx, 13, 64, 16, gui_scale_idx, "Fit|100%|125%|150%|200%",
      gui_scale_idx == 0 ? "Fit"  : gui_scale_idx == 1 ? "100%" :
      gui_scale_idx == 2 ? "125%" : gui_scale_idx == 3 ? "150%" : "200%");
g_i != gui_scale_idx ? ( gui_scale_idx = g_i; gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] = g_i + 1; );

gk_button(41, g_rr - 18, 13, 18, 16, help_open, "?") ? (
  help_open = !help_open;
  help_open && !help_lines ? help_lines = gk_help_load(HELP_FILE, HELP_BASE, HELP_MAX);
);

// Freeze the controls underneath while the help is open, keeping the two
// header controls live so the help can be closed again.
help_open ? gk_help_input(40, 41);

// ================= scrolling display =================
// Plotted in dB, FLOORDB at the centre line and 0 dB at the edges. A linear
// amplitude plot - which is what this used to be - collapses everything below
// about -30 dB onto the centre line, and a threshold down at -60 spends its
// whole life in that region. dB also means Threshold and Ceiling can be drawn
// on the same scale as the waveform instead of being invisible.
accent(0.149, 0.784, 0.902);   // cyan
gk_nine(gk_img_wave_frame, 16, 8, 10, 40, 530, 160);
gk_wave_geom(10, 40, 530, 160);
g_ix = gk_wv_ix; g_iy = gk_wv_iy; g_iw = gk_wv_iw; g_ih = gk_wv_ih;
g_cy = gk_wv_cy; g_half = gk_wv_half;
g_yscale = g_half/(-FLOORDB);          // device px per dB, from the centre out

// One pass, and each drawn column takes the MAX over every history slot it
// covers. 1024 slots into ~500 pixels means point-sampling would silently
// drop every other transient, which made the display disagree with the ear.
g_iwi = floor(g_iw);
g_i = 0;
g_have = 0;
loop(g_iwi,
  g_a = floor(g_i*HIST_N/g_iwi);
  g_b = floor((g_i+1)*HIST_N/g_iwi);
  g_b <= g_a ? ( g_b = g_a + 1; );

  g_din = FLOORDB - 200; g_dout = FLOORDB - 200; g_dgr = 0; g_drt = 1;
  g_k = g_a;
  while (g_k < g_b) (
    g_p = (hpos + g_k) & HMASK;
    g_din  = max(g_din,  HIST_IN[g_p]);
    g_dout = max(g_dout, HIST_OUT[g_p]);
    g_dgr  = max(g_dgr,  HIST_GR[g_p]);
    g_drt  = max(g_drt,  HIST_RT[g_p]);
    g_k += 1;
  );

  g_hin  = min(g_half, max(0, (g_din  - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
  g_hout = min(g_half, max(0, (g_dout - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
  g_x = g_ix + g_i;
  // older columns fade out, the same gesture gk_wave_draw makes
  g_fade = 0.4 + 0.6*g_i/g_iwi;
  // draw the shorter trace last so it stays visible inside the taller one.
  // The input is the translucent one: where the two overlap it tints the
  // output rather than hiding it, so the grey standing outside the cyan - the
  // gain reduction - is the thing the eye lands on.
  g_hout >= g_hin ? (
    g_hout > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,0.90*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hout, g_x, g_cy+g_hout); );
    g_hin  > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.45,0.52,0.62,0.55*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hin,  g_x, g_cy+g_hin);  );
  ) : (
    g_hin  > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.45,0.52,0.62,0.55*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hin,  g_x, g_cy+g_hin);  );
    g_hout > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,0.90*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hout, g_x, g_cy+g_hout); );
  );

  // Gain reduction, hanging from the top edge: translucent fill with a solid
  // bottom edge, so it reads over the waveform rather than hiding it. The
  // scale is FIXED at 0..GR_FULL_DB and does not follow any slider.
  g_hh = min(g_dgr/GR_FULL_DB, 1)*g_ih;
  g_hh > 0 ? (
    gfx_set(0.941,0.588,0.157,0.22*g_fade);
    gfx_line(g_x, g_iy, g_x, g_iy+g_hh);
    gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,0.95*g_fade);
    gfx_rect(g_x, g_iy+g_hh, 1, max(1, gk_S));
  );

  // Ratio, as a line rising from the bottom edge, log-scaled to Max Ratio. A
  // line rather than a fill: two filled overlays on one plot and neither is
  // readable.
  g_ry = g_iy + g_ih - min(log(max(g_drt,1))/LN_RATIO, 1)*g_ih;
  gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,0.85*g_fade);
  g_have ? gfx_line(g_x, g_ry, g_px, g_py);
  g_px = g_x; g_py = g_ry; g_have = 1;

  g_i += 1;
);

// centre line, then the two level references mirrored about it
gfx_set(0.30,0.30,0.34,1); gfx_line(g_ix, g_cy, g_ix+g_iw, g_cy);

// threshold (dotted) - where compression begins
g_ht = min(g_half, max(0,(g_thr - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
gfx_set(0.70,0.70,0.76,0.85);
g_i = 0; loop(floor(g_iw/8),
  gfx_line(g_ix+g_i*8, g_cy-g_ht, g_ix+g_i*8+3, g_cy-g_ht);
  gfx_line(g_ix+g_i*8, g_cy+g_ht, g_ix+g_i*8+3, g_cy+g_ht);
  g_i += 1;
);

// ceiling - the thing being guaranteed, so it gets the solid line
g_hc = min(g_half, max(0,(g_ceil - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
gfx_set(0.941,0.784,0.235,0.9);
gfx_line(g_ix, g_cy-g_hc, g_ix+g_iw, g_cy-g_hc);
gfx_line(g_ix, g_cy+g_hc, g_ix+g_iw, g_cy+g_hc);

// "now" edge, from the kit, so this display matches the other Magnolius plots
gfx_getimgdim(gk_img_playhead_line, g_sw, g_sh);
g_sw > 0 ? gfx_blit(gk_img_playhead_line,1,0, 0,0,g_sw,g_sh, g_ix+g_iw-3*gk_S, g_iy, 6*gk_S, g_ih);
gfx_getimgdim(gk_img_playhead_marker, g_sw, g_sh);
g_sw > 0 ? gfx_blit(gk_img_playhead_marker,1,0, 0,0,g_sw,g_sh, g_ix+g_iw-7*gk_S, g_iy+g_ih-6*gk_S, 14*gk_S, 10*gk_S);

// Legend, inside the frame: each word in the colour of the thing it names,
// which is the only thing that says which trace is which. Placed exactly as
// Upward Compressor places its own - absolute logical coordinates off the
// window, font 1, y = 45, first word at x = 18 - so the two plugins read the
// same in a chain. The gaps between words are 6.5 to 9 px in 11 px Arial;
// re-measure if gk_font ever changes.
//
// It does get a backdrop, which Upward does not need: there the LIFT overlay
// grows from the BOTTOM edge, while the gain-reduction overlay here hangs from
// the TOP and prints straight through the words whenever the compressor is
// working hard - which is exactly when someone is reading them.
gfx_setfont(1);
gfx_set(0.04,0.045,0.05,0.72);
gfx_rect(14*gk_S, 43*gk_S, 178*gk_S, 15*gk_S);
gfx_set(0.45,0.52,0.62,1);    gfx_x =  18*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("IN");
gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,1);    gfx_x =  38*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("OUT");
gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,1); gfx_x =  68*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("GR");
gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,1);    gfx_x =  91*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("RATIO");
gfx_set(0.70,0.70,0.76,1);    gfx_x = 133*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("THR");
gfx_set(0.941,0.784,0.235,1); gfx_x = 163*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("CEIL");

// ================= transfer curve =================
// The maths below is deliberately a copy of the @sample curve: @gfx must not
// share a function with the audio thread. Keep the two in step. It is drawn
// from the LIVE solved ratio, so the curve bends as the plugin works - that
// is the whole point of a compressor that derives its own ratio.
gk_panel(550, 40, 160, 160);
gk_header(0, 550, 40, 160, 22, "CURVE", -1, 0);
// The plot fills the panel down to the same 8 px pad it has on either side.
// It used to stop 12 px short to leave a line for an axis caption; with the
// caption gone the plot takes that space rather than leaving the panel
// bottom-heavy.
g_qx = 558*gk_S; g_qy = 68*gk_S; g_qw = 144*gk_S; g_qh = 124*gk_S;
g_span = CURVE_HI - CURVE_LO;                 // dB spanned vertically

gfx_set(0.08,0.085,0.095,1); gfx_rect(g_qx,g_qy,g_qw,g_qh);
gfx_set(0.20,0.20,0.23,1);
g_i = 0; loop(5,
  gfx_line(g_qx, g_qy+g_qh*g_i*0.25, g_qx+g_qw, g_qy+g_qh*g_i*0.25);
  gfx_line(g_qx+g_qw*g_i*0.25, g_qy, g_qx+g_qw*g_i*0.25, g_qy+g_qh);
  g_i += 1;
);

// unity reference: what the input would do untouched
gfx_set(0.40,0.40,0.44,1);
gfx_line(g_qx, g_qy+g_qh, g_qx+g_qw, g_qy+g_qh - (-CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span);

// threshold, as a vertical marker on the input axis
g_tx = g_qx + (g_thr-CURVE_LO)*g_qw/(-CURVE_LO);
gfx_set(0.70,0.70,0.76,0.8); gfx_line(g_tx, g_qy, g_tx, g_qy+g_qh);

// ceiling, as a horizontal marker on the output axis. Output gain moves the
// line with the curve, because the output really does land at ceiling + gain.
g_cl = g_qy + g_qh - (g_ceil + s_comp - CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span;
gfx_set(0.941,0.784,0.235,0.65);
g_i = 0; loop(floor(g_qw/8),
  gfx_line(g_qx+g_i*8, g_cl, g_qx+g_i*8+4, g_cl);
  g_i += 1;
);

gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,1);
g_i = 0;
loop(121,
  g_ld = CURVE_LO + g_i*0.5;

  // static curve at the ratio the plugin is holding right now
  g_gg = g_ld > g_thr ? exp(-(g_ld - g_thr)*(1 - 1/max(rs,1))*LN10_20) : 1;

  // plot what actually leaves the plugin: blend first, then output gain
  g_fac = (1 - g_blend) + g_blend*g_gg;
  g_ov  = g_ld + log(max(g_fac, TINY))*DB20 + s_comp;

  g_xx = g_qx + (g_ld-CURVE_LO)*g_qw/(-CURVE_LO);
  g_yy = max(g_qy, min(g_qy+g_qh, g_qy + g_qh - (g_ov-CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span));
  g_i == 0 ? ( gfx_x = g_xx; gfx_y = g_yy; ) : gfx_lineto(g_xx,g_yy,1);
  g_i += 1;
);

// ================= dynamics =================
gk_panel(10, 210, 248, 246);
gk_header(0, 10, 210, 248, 22, "DYNAMICS", -1, 0);
accent(0.149, 0.784, 0.902);   // cyan

g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(10, 0,  22, 240, 64,  3, -60, 0, -18);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(11, 0,  98, 240, 64,  2, -24, 0, -0.3);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(12, 0, 174, 240, 64,  9, 1, 10, 3);
gk_text_c( 54, 306, "Threshold", 1);
gk_text_c(130, 306, "Ceiling", 1);
gk_text_c(206, 306, "Max Ratio", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f dB", s_thr);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(50,  24, 320, 60, 16,  3, -60, 0, -18, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.2f dB", s_ceil);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(51, 100, 320, 60, 16,  2, -24, 0, -0.3, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f:1", s_maxr);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(52, 176, 320, 60, 16,  9, 1, 10, 3, #s);

g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(13, 0,  22, 348, 64,  8, 10, 2000, 200);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(14, 0,  98, 348, 64,  7, 0.5, 20, 5);
gk_text_c( 54, 414, "Ratio Release", 1);
gk_text_c(130, 414, "Lookahead", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.0f ms", s_rel);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(53,  24, 428, 60, 16,  8, 10, 2000, 200, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f ms", s_la);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(54, 100, 428, 60, 16,  7, 0.5, 20, 5, #s);

// ================= gain =================
// Signal order, left to right: drive in, how much of the compressed signal to
// keep, level out. Input Gain is a genuine pre-compressor drive - the detector,
// the curve and the dry half of the blend all see it - while Output Gain is a
// plain trim after everything.
gk_panel(268, 210, 248, 246);
gk_header(0, 268, 210, 248, 22, "GAIN", -1, 0);
accent(0.941, 0.784, 0.235);   // yellow

// Alt-drag links the two gains in opposite directions - see the block after
// the fields. Both values are captured before any widget can move them.
g_in0 = s_in; g_out0 = s_comp;

g_cin   = gk_knob_slider(20, 1, 280, 240, 64,  1, -12, 24, 0);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(22, 1, 356, 240, 64,  4, 0, 100, 100);
g_cout  = gk_knob_slider(21, 2, 432, 240, 64,  5, -20, 20, 0);
gk_text_c(312, 306, "Input Gain", 1);
gk_text_c(388, 306, "Blend", 1);
gk_text_c(464, 306, "Output Gain", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%+.1f dB", s_in);
g_cin  |= gk_field_edit_slider(56, 282, 320, 60, 16,  1, -12, 24, 0, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.0f %%", s_blend);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(58, 358, 320, 60, 16,  4, 0, 100, 100, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%+.1f dB", s_comp);
g_cout |= gk_field_edit_slider(57, 434, 320, 60, 16,  5, -20, 20, 0, #s);

// Alt-drag on either gain moves the other one the opposite way by the same
// number of dB: drive harder into the compressor and take the same amount
// back out, so what changes is how hard it works and not the level. That is a
// real difference here - Input Gain is part of the solve and Output Gain is
// not - so the pair is worth one gesture. It reads the delta the widgets just
// produced rather than duplicating their drag maths, so it covers the knob,
// the wheel and the value field alike - and it is gated on those widgets' own
// change flags, so an automation write landing mid-frame cannot be mistaken
// for a drag. Ctrl is excluded because a reset is a jump to a default, not a
// gesture with a delta worth mirroring. Each value
// still clamps to its own range, so the link goes one-sided once either end
// runs out of travel.
g_chg |= g_cin | g_cout;
(mouse_cap & 16) && !(mouse_cap & 4) ? (
  g_cin ? (
    s_comp = gk_clamp(g_out0 - (s_in - g_in0), -20, 20);
    slider_automate(2^4);
  ) : g_cout ? (
    s_in = gk_clamp(g_in0 - (s_comp - g_out0), -12, 24);
    slider_automate(2^0);
  );
);

// Display History is a display control, not a gain one, and it sits here only
// to give both panels the same two-row grid - GAIN had one row against
// DYNAMICS' two and read as half empty. It takes the section's yellow so the
// panel stays one accent, per the kit's rule.
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(15, 1, 280, 348, 64, 11, 1, 30, 6);
gk_text_c(312, 414, "History", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f s", s_hist);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(55, 282, 428, 60, 16, 11, 1, 30, 6, #s);

// ================= meters =================
// Live data, so both bars are drawn with primitives rather than blitted.
gk_panel(526, 210, 184, 246);
gk_header(0, 526, 210, 184, 22, "METERS", -1, 0);
accent(0.941, 0.588, 0.157);   // orange

g_my = 244*gk_S; g_mh = 160*gk_S;
g_g1 = 559*gk_S; g_g2 = 643*gk_S; g_mw = 32*gk_S;

// tracks
gfx_set(0.08,0.085,0.095,1);
gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my, g_mw, g_mh);
gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my, g_mw, g_mh);

// quarter ticks across both
gfx_set(0.22,0.22,0.25,1);
g_i = 1; loop(3,
  gfx_line(g_g1, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25, g_g1+g_mw, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25);
  gfx_line(g_g2, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25, g_g2+g_mw, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25);
  g_i += 1;
);

// gain reduction: grows DOWNWARD from the top, the same direction as the
// overlay on the plot above, so the two read as one gesture
g_bh = min(gr_db/GR_FULL_DB, 1)*g_mh;
g_bh > 0 ? (
  gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,0.85); gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my, g_mw, g_bh);
  gfx_set(1.0,0.78,0.35,1); gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my+g_bh-max(1,gk_S), g_mw, max(1,gk_S));
);

// ratio: grows UPWARD from 1:1 at the bottom to RATIO_FULL at the top, on a
// log scale that does NOT follow Max Ratio - see the constant.
g_bh = min(log(max(rt_now,1))/LN_RATIO, 1)*g_mh;
g_bh > 0 ? (
  gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,0.85); gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my+g_mh-g_bh, g_mw, g_bh);
  gfx_set(0.62,1.0,0.78,1); gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my+g_mh-g_bh, g_mw, max(1,gk_S));
);

// frames last, so the fills sit inside them
gfx_set(0.28,0.28,0.32,1);
gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my, g_mw, g_mh, 0);
gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my, g_mw, g_mh, 0);

// End-of-scale captions, outside the bars: without them neither meter says
// what "full" means, and Max Ratio changes what it means on the right-hand one.
// Both columns sit to the LEFT of their bar and are RIGHT-aligned to it, so a
// four-digit Max Ratio grows into the gap instead of off the panel - at 1000
// a left-aligned caption on the right of the bar overran the panel edge.
gfx_setfont(1);
gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 1);
sprintf(#s, "0");
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g1 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my; gfx_drawstr(#s);
sprintf(#s, "%d", GR_FULL_DB);
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g1 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my + g_mh - g_th; gfx_drawstr(#s);
sprintf(#s, "%d", RATIO_FULL);
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g2 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my; gfx_drawstr(#s);
sprintf(#s, "1");
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g2 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my + g_mh - g_th; gfx_drawstr(#s);

gk_text_c(575, 408, "GR", 1);
gk_text_c(659, 408, "RATIO", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f dB", gr_db);
gk_field(538, 424, 74, 18, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f:1", rt_now);
gk_field(622, 424, 74, 18, #s);

help_open ? gk_help_draw(10, 40, 700, 424, "Help  -  Variable Ratio Compressor", HELP_BASE, help_lines);

// Wordmark, bottom right, its right edge on the same 10 px margin as the
// panels. LOGO_ASPECT is the source's width/height; a 1-frame strip is just a
// scaled blit that respects gk_S. The version sits opposite it, so a stale
// compiled instance is visible at a glance - REAPER caches compiled JSFX per
// FX instance.
gk_strip(11, 1, 0, UI_W - 10 - 14*LOGO_ASPECT, UI_H - 24, 14*LOGO_ASPECT, 14);
gk_text_c(30, UI_H - 22, "v1.2", 1);

g_bad = count_missing_images(12);
g_bad > 0 ? (
  gfx_set(1, 0.42, 0.38, 1);
  gfx_x = 300*gk_S; gfx_y = 15*gk_S;
  gfx_drawstr("images failed to load: ");
  gfx_drawnumber(g_bad, 0);
);

// One flag, picked up by @block on the audio thread.
g_chg ? need_update = 1;
gk_end();

@serialize
// GUI-only state, so it is serialised rather than made a slider. Append-only:
// a new value goes AFTER this one or every project saved with the old order
// reads back wrong.
file_var(0, gui_scale_idx);
